  "register": "notes",
  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nNo Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions\n\nChief Complaint:\nRecurrence of AML\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nMr. ___ is a ___ gentleman who was initially \ndiagnosed with AML likely arising from MDS in ___ s/p 7+3 \ninducton followed by a myeloablative matched related donor  \nallogenic stem cell transplant with bu/cy conditioning on \n___ and he has been in disease remission since \nthis time and without evidence of graft-versus-host disease. He \nunfortunately developed a right distal leg mass that was \ngranulocytic sarcoma, s/p XRT and DLI. Restaging revealed L \noptic nerve and facial nerve enhancement followed serially and \nrelatively stable. Recently admitted for expedited work up of \npalpable 2 ~1cm lumps in face near facial nerve which revealed \nAML. Now electively admitted for chemotherapy.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ h/o AML s/p MRD ___, found to have localized relapsed \ndisease ___ presenting as right calf granulocytic \nsarcoma s/p radiation and DLI now with recurrent AML in L \nmaxillary mass.\n\n# Recurrent AML: Allongenic transplant from sibling donor in \n___. Had recurrence as R calf granulocytic sarcoma. During \nrestaging revealed L optic nerve and facial nerve enhancement \nwhich had been followed with serial MRIs and had been stable. \nPatient recently admitted for expedited work up of now palpable \nL facial mass, concerning for involvement of L facial nerve. FNA \nrevealed AML cells. On last admission ENT was consulted and \nthere was concern that patient can not entirely close L eye. \nTherefore they recommended eye drops and lacrilube qHS. CN exam \nnotable for mild left facial droop, all other CN intact. CXR and \nTTE wnl. LP performed ___ and did not have enough cells to run \nflow. Bone marrow bx performed ___, results showed normal \nappearing marrow. MEC (___). On ___, exam demonstrated \nhorizontal nystagmus but this improved after finishing MEC. \nFollowing MEC, the patient's counts began to improve, and after \nhe was no longer neutropenic he was able to be discharged home. \nHe will have followup in ___ clinic with possible future \nplan for donor lymphocyte infusion from brother.\n\n# Febrile Neutropenia: Patient became neutropenic on ___ and \nwas started on ciprofloxacin and fluconazole prophylactically. \nHe then developed Fever on ___ to  100.5 and was started on \nVanc/Cefepime, but was otherwise hemodynamically stable. Given \ncontinued fever while on Vanc/Cefepime on ___ to 101.2 patient \nwas started on voriconazole and a CT chest was obtained which \nwas showed innumerable, small hazy pulmonary nodular opacities \nin a miliary pattern c/f infectious etiology including fungal. \nPatient was switched from voriconazole to amphotericin and pt \nseen by ID and pulm on ___. ID recommended linezolid for \npossible nocardia infection, but it was decided, given clinical \nstability, to hold off until after studies from bronchoscopy \nwere obtained. Pt underwent uncomplicated bronchoscopy on ___ \nwhich showed normal airway, and all studies were negative. Pt \ndeveloped headaches which prompted MRI (see below) which \nrevealed acute sinusitis and was therefore started on flagyl on \n___. B-glucan and galactomanan negative. A repeat CT chest on \n___ showed no change in the size of his opacities. The \npatient's counts began to improve, and after he was no longer \nneutropenic, his vanc, flagyl, and cefepime were stopped, and he \nwas transitioned to levofloxacin. He was discharged on \nposaconazole and levofloxacin, which he will at least take until \nhis followup appointment with ID, at which time his final course \ncan be determined.\n\n# Altered mental status: On evening of ___, patient was noted \nto have slowing of mental status. Neurological exam was intact \nexcept for a +Rhomberg sign. Patient also showed signs of \ndecreased executive function and was unable to perform serial 7s \nor spell \"WORLD\" backwards. Also noted to have some personality \nchanges from family. Recent MRI did not show any gross \nstructural changes or enhancement of the meninges to suggest \ninflammation which is further supported by his lack of nuchal \nrigidity. Neurology consult noted attentional deficits, \ndiminished proprioception, vibratory sense and pinprick \nsensation in addition to difficulty with tandem gait. The \npatient's oxycodone was stopped, and his symptoms improved. \n\n#Headache: On ___ patient was complaining of unilateral \nheadache over the R eye, midface and frontotemporal region. CN \nexam wnl except for stable L facial droop. MRI was obtained \nwhich showed new b/l R>L sinusitis and no intracranial \npathology. As above, he was started on flagyl, which was stopped \nafter his counts improved. \n\n#Tinnitus: Patient complaining of mild tinnitus in L ear with \ndecreased hearing on left side. Not a known side effect of \npatient's MEC therapy or his antibiotics. However there is less \nspecific neurotoxicity listed as a side effect for multiple \nmedications. Likely due to sinusitis. Exam revealed copious \ncerumen which may also be contributing.\n\n#Hematuria: Patient had an episode of gross hematuria on evening \nof ___ along with a mild episode of epistaxis. Plts were found \nto be 11. Other coags and fibrinogen wnl. Patient was started on \n120cc/hr NS and given 1u of plt with good response. Pt had good \nUOP overnight with resolution of hematuria. \n\n#Nausea: Pt now c/o nausea. Was switched from odansetron to \nolanzapine was more sedated. Given previous relief of symptoms \nwith odansetron, it was restarted with QTc monitoring given \nconcomitant use of cipro and fluc. QTc: 405 (___)\n\n#History of Pulmonary GVHD: Currently asymptomatic. Continued \nhome budesonide.\n\n#Attention Deficit disorder: Continued home \ndextroamphetamine-amphetamine 10 mg oral BID.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPrimary Diagnoses:\n- Recurrent AML\n- Febrile Neutropenia\n\nSecondary Diagnoses:\n- headache\n- benign prostatic hyperplasia\n- pulmonary opacities\n- Attention deficit disorder",
